From: Francois Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: do not disable Rx NAPI twice

edma_cfg_rx_napi_delete() calls napi_disable() on every NAPI it deletes,
and both of its callers, the edma_hw_configure() error unwind and
edma_destroy(), have already called edma_cfg_rx_napi_disable() on the
same instances immediately before.

napi_disable() sets NAPI_STATE_SCHED and NAPI_STATE_NPSVC when it
succeeds, and the next call waits for exactly those bits to clear:

	while (val & (NAPIF_STATE_SCHED | NAPIF_STATE_NPSVC)) {
		usleep_range(20, 200);
		val = READ_ONCE(n->state);
	}

Nothing clears them until the NAPI is enabled again, so the second
disable spins forever.  Removing the module, or unwinding a failed
edma_hw_configure(), hangs in the delete helper with the rings still
allocated and their buffers still mapped.

Delete the NAPI without disabling it again.  Both callers disable first,
and the Rx NAPIs are enabled in the statement that follows the one that
adds them, so there is no path that reaches the delete with a NAPI that
was never enabled.

Signed-off-by: Francois Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_cfg_rx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_cfg_rx.c
@@ -927,7 +927,6 @@ void edma_cfg_rx_napi_delete(void)
 		if (!rxdesc_ring->napi_added)
 			continue;
 
-		napi_disable(&rxdesc_ring->napi);
 		netif_napi_del(&rxdesc_ring->napi);
 		rxdesc_ring->napi_added = false;
 	}
